 Tests cover clean install and EULA persistence, offline restart, interrupted guide page restoration, completing without losing settings, old accepted profiles and invalid JSON. This is not yet native Windows keyboard-only/accessibility approval or a complete screenshot-based Python/PyQt visual parity check. Issue #41 remains open until those user-facing gates are verified.
+
+## Guide navigation durability and visual smoke (D38)
+
+Navigation to another Guide page and changing Guide level are executed through the same Settings Owner as completion. The running GUI now explicitly awaits `SettingsApi::flush()` for each navigation command before reporting that the change was saved; a persistence failure displays a visible Guide notice instead of a settings-only error behind the modal. The presenter changes Guide level and remaps the current page to the closest visible page **in one settings revision**, not as two separately persisted updates. Subsequent Core snapshots synchronize both level and page with the GUI. If the runtime is not available or its command queue is closed, navigation does not advance optimistically as though it were saved.
+
+The `gui_snapshot` example supports `onboarding-eula-ru`, `onboarding-eula-en`, `onboarding-guide-ru`, and `onboarding-guide-en` visual fixtures. These use real bundled Slint components and source-derived EULA/Guide text, but are **rendering smoke only**: the example does not start the desktop Core or prove live input/keyboard accessibility. A hard process crash before a successful flush acknowledgement still cannot guarantee that an already-visible optimistic page is durable; filesystem crash consistency and actual Windows GUI interaction require separate acceptance. The full Python/PyQt visual-parity gate remains open.
